From: Joel Gerber <Jitsusama@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] Fill the fullest chunks first after a sweep

A sweep sorted each chunk's free runs onto the front of the bins in the
order the chunks happened to lie, so the chunks sorted in last supplied
allocation first whatever they held. What lived long was scattered over
chunks that were nearly empty, and none of them emptied enough to be
freed. In five hours the shell's heap went from 83 MB held around 41
live to 284 MB around 72, and of that 284, 135 MB was free slots on
pages still holding something live. The chunks a sweep keeps are now
sorted in by the slots they use, the fullest last, so what's made next
fills them, and the chunks using little are left to empty for a later
sweep to free.

That holds in the last bin too. A small request that no smaller bin
can serve takes its head and bumps through the rest, so the head should
be the fullest of the chunks with a long run. A request for a long run
passes at most a few runs there and may take a new chunk, but it fills
at least half of it. The list the sort uses is the allocator's, kept
from sweep to sweep, so a sweep allocates nothing once it's grown, and
ties go by address, so the order is the same each time.

---
--- a/src/qml/memory/qv4mm.cpp
+++ b/src/qml/memory/qv4mm.cpp
@@ -553,10 +553,25 @@ void BlockAllocator::sweep()
 
     usedSlotsAfterLastSweep = 0;
 
+    // Each chunk's runs go on the front of their bins, so the chunk sorted in last is the one
+    // allocation fills first. Sorted in by what they use, the fullest last, what's made next
+    // fills the chunks nearly full already, and the chunks using little are left to empty, for a
+    // later sweep to free, rather than each keeping a few of what's made and none ever freed.
+    // That holds in every bin, the last too: a small request no smaller bin can serve takes the
+    // last bin's head and bumps through what's left of it, which should be the fullest chunk of
+    // those with a long run, not the emptiest. A request of a long run passes at most a few runs
+    // too short for it there, and may take a new chunk, which it fills at least half of. The
+    // list is the allocator's, kept from sweep to sweep, so a sweep allocates nothing once it
+    // has grown, and ties go by address, so the order is the same each time.
+    byUse.clear();
     std::for_each(chunks.begin(), firstEmptyChunk, [this](Chunk *c) {
-        c->sortIntoBins(freeBins, NumBins);
-        usedSlotsAfterLastSweep += c->nUsedSlots();
+        byUse.emplace_back(c->nUsedSlots(), c);
     });
+    std::sort(byUse.begin(), byUse.end());
+    for (const auto &[used, c] : byUse) {
+        c->sortIntoBins(freeBins, NumBins);
+        usedSlotsAfterLastSweep += used;
+    }
 
     // only free the chunks at the end to avoid that the sweep() calls indirectly
     // access freed memory
--- a/src/qml/memory/qv4mm_p.h
+++ b/src/qml/memory/qv4mm_p.h
@@ -230,6 +230,9 @@ struct BlockAllocator {
     ChunkAllocator *chunkAllocator;
     ExecutionEngine *engine;
     std::vector<Chunk *> chunks;
+    // The chunks a sweep keeps, by the slots each uses, kept between sweeps so sorting them
+    // allocates nothing once it has grown.
+    std::vector<std::pair<std::size_t, Chunk *>> byUse;
     uint *allocationStats = nullptr;
 };
 
